{"records":[{"id":"xn3vv2cdnk","title":"High-throughput CRISPR screens to dissect macrophage-Shigella interactions","description":"The positive hits, negative hits, and non-targeting controls of genome-wide CRISPR screens; Supplemental Tables.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-23T06:18:30.182Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xn3vv2cdnk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xn3vv2cdnk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"LAI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetic Screening","Host-Pathogen Interaction","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hfbhym45fz","title":"CRISPR screening reveals a requirement for ribosome recycling in efficient SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting and viral replication. Rehfeld et al.","description":"Supplemental Table S1. Results of SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting genome-wide CRISPR screens (MAGeCK analysis).\nSupplemental Table S2. Oligonucleotides.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-19T12:17:58.922Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hfbhym45fz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hfbhym45fz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Mendell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frederick","last_name":"Rehfeld","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0003xa228","name":"Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas","grant_id":"RP220309"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00np6vq88","name":"Welch Foundation","grant_id":"I-1961-20210327"},{"id":"https://ror.org/006w34k90","name":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Oligonucleotide","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k6wm46g4tw","title":"Data set for the paper entitled \"CRISPR-enabled genetic screens identify synthetic lethal targets across frequently altered cancer drivers\" by Desjardins, Bowlan, et al. (2026)","description":"We undertook a series of genome-wide CRISPR screens using functionally validated isogenic cell lines and mapped SL interactions across 15 genetic alterations characteristic of diseases with high incidence and unmet clinical need: FBXW7, CCNE1, CDK12, ARID1A, KMT2D, DNMT3A, TET2, KEAP1, STK11, IDH1, SF3B1, SRSF2, U2AF1, chromosome 18q loss, and chromosome 13q loss. The data deposited here make the raw read count files available for all CRISPR screens conducted in the study. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-26T11:43:11.208Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k6wm46g4tw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k6wm46g4tw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jordan","last_name":"Young","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alejandro","last_name":"Álvarez-Quilón","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Desjardins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julian","last_name":"Bowlan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nxvdzybwhf","title":"Illuminating host-mycobacterial interactions with genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screens","description":"The data deposited here contains genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screen data, including the positive hits, the negative hits, and non-targeting controls. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-30T11:32:01.108Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxvdzybwhf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxvdzybwhf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","High-Throughput Sequencing","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z4ht3txn2k","title":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023","description":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023. This dataset contains Source and additional Data for all figures.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-06T07:16:19.286Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z4ht3txn2k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z4ht3txn2k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yichao","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":null}],"categories":["DNA Replication","DNA Repair","Flow Cytometry","Cancer Chemotherapy","DNA Damage","Functional Genomics","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gfcn2wmrpf","title":"Dataset for the paper entitled \"A genetic map of the response to DNA damage in human cells\" by Olivieri M et al. Cell 2020","description":"We used pooled CRISPR/Cas9 screens in the human RPE1-hTERT p53-/- cell line against 27 genotoxic agents. The Dataset herein make the primary data available. The files are:\n\n* Additional genes of interest (discussion and data relating to TMEM2, ESD, USP37, PHF12, BTAF1 and DRAP1)\n* Supplementary Raw Data: Data used to make all the graphs in the manuscript\n* Folder \"CRISPR screen readcount files\". Readcounts for the screens undertaken as part of this study.\n* Folder \"Raw images for immunoblots\": uncropped images for all the blots in the manuscript\n* Folder: \"Additional QC analyses of CRISPR screens\": Additional analyses that measure the quality of the screens using Presion-Recall curves of essential genes and an estimate of genes that scored as hits due to batch effects.\n* Folder \"Data_Analysis_Code\" R markdown file along with data files\n* File: \"Additional Genes of Interests\" contains a short discussion of genes we found noteworthy after analysis of our screen data.\n\nVersion 2 note: this version differs from Version 1 by a single file (FDRPos_31screens.csv) which has a tab error fixed.\n\n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-07T14:26:40.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gfcn2wmrpf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gfcn2wmrpf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Olivieri","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Quality","Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":30945,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":54,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":30945,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":30896,"restricted":14,"embargoed":35},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":427},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":179},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":168},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":129},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":120},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":107},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":95},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":94},{"id":"https://ror.org/00djwmt25","name":"University Grants Committee","count":77},{"id":"https://ror.org/059ex5q34","name":"Secretaría de Ciencia, Humanidades, Tecnología e Innovación","count":56}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":30338},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":432},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":34},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":29},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":27},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":22},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":22},